ldirectord: accept IPv4-mapped IPv6 literals consistently (IPv6-first)

Saphira is IPv6-first, and this file was not internally consistent about
IPv6 address syntax. The configuration-file parsers accept a dot inside a
bracketed address -- `[0-9A-Fa-f:.]' -- but eleven places used the dotless
class `[0-9A-Fa-f:]', which cannot match an IPv4-mapped or
IPv4-compatible IPv6 literal.

Eleven occurrences, in three groups and two very different failure modes.

GROUP 1 -- the parsers for `ipvsadm -Ln' output (8 places, in
ld_read_ipvsadm and ld_read_ipvsadm_thresholds). Reproduced against
ldirectord's own real-server regex:

    192.0.2.11:80            MATCH
    [2001:db8::1]:80         MATCH
    [::1]:80                 MATCH
    [::ffff:192.0.2.11]:80   NO MATCH

The consequence is quiet and cumulative. Such a real server parses
correctly out of ldirectord.cf, but when ipvsadm echoes it back the parse
falls through to the `else' arm and logs "Unknown line read from ipvsadm"
at debug level 2 only. The server is therefore absent from %oldsrv,
ldirectord never concludes it is already configured, and it re-adds the
same real server on every sync interval, forever. No error appears above
debug level 2, so the only symptom is repeated churn in the IPVS table --
and the service may well still forward traffic throughout, while
ldirectord believes it is misconfigured and rewrites the table under it.

GROUP 2 -- _ld_read_config_virtual_resolve (1 place). This one is worse
than churn: it is the VIRTUAL service path, and the fallthrough is
`split /:/, $ip_port'. Reproduced:

    [2001:db8::1]:80         -> server='[2001:db8::1]'  port=80
    [::ffff:192.0.2.100]:80  -> server='['             port=(empty)

An IPv4-mapped virtual address is not merely unparsed, it is MANGLED: the
address is split on every colon, yielding the single-character server "["
and an empty port. ldirectord then programs a garbage IPVS virtual
service. This is the most severe IPv6 defect found in the package, and it
is reachable from ordinary configuration.

GROUP 3 -- the real-server config dispatch that distinguishes an IPv6
literal from an IPv4 one (2 places, in ld_read_config). An
IPv4-mapped address there is classified with the wrong address family
and then rejected as a malformed IPv6 literal.

This patch makes the character class uniform. Adding a dot to a hex
character class cannot make it match anything that was not already a
valid IPv6 literal, so IPv4, pure IPv6 and IPv4-mapped forms all parse,
and every site now agrees with the config-side parsers that were already
correct.

